{"authored" [{"question" "q-117662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x+5, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+13, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}(\\frac{1}{3}x+13)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+5x){|}_{-1}^{3}+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+13x){|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =[3\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+5(3)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-5(-1)]+[\\frac{{9}^{2}}{6}+13(9)-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{6}-13(3)] \\\\  & =[12+20]+[12+78] \\\\  & =32+90 \\\\  & =122.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8ddb95531a97f38217f742fcfc2b96f34ca54152b25c7ff23e8ac70cfc220ca1" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "101"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1115"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "122"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "127"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "90"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "122"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/bac953cd2389ef36b415dcdce87730f244958ef0fe2f67911878c5cb3bfcafc6.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,4],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [4,6].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,6].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =8+4-1 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8ddb95531a97f38217f742fcfc2b96f34ca54152b25c7ff23e8ac70cfc220ca1" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "13"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/state.json" "file_sha256" "b19b19cff5d511839d0ad8399e234f3685ea81e54dd140d297072353c5c11f55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-432.json" "file_sha256" "56e94a6e43f7791b16ce88d775bd5d24166385feb3298658bf6fba5ab6cec906" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x{|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =[3(0)-3(-2)]+[({5}^{2}+3(5))-({0}^{2}+3(0))] \\\\  & =6+25+15 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-432.json" "file_sha256" "56e94a6e43f7791b16ce88d775bd5d24166385feb3298658bf6fba5ab6cec906" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-433.json" "file_sha256" "92da86a3ba3155597b55c6fa9c94e1533ad1e5a117d41cc23532b4a74747de55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [0,2)$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-433.json" "file_sha256" "92da86a3ba3155597b55c6fa9c94e1533ad1e5a117d41cc23532b4a74747de55" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-434.json" "file_sha256" "519c2e5e20e1150e255fe2990fc53f0ca2bc38daa83cf9a835773db656b47db7" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/example-434.json" "file_sha256" "519c2e5e20e1150e255fe2990fc53f0ca2bc38daa83cf9a835773db656b47db7" "basis" 1151 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/assets/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "596a51becfd398064bc85416a015a323234394e5ede30ceeb664d979d795b403" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a right triange, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+(4-\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "596a51becfd398064bc85416a015a323234394e5ede30ceeb664d979d795b403" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ea52c52978867eeda2b16feac3f8ce66b2eb424c0df4713f4f31b2f85929d29c" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "596a51becfd398064bc85416a015a323234394e5ede30ceeb664d979d795b403" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4-\\pi "] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "596a51becfd398064bc85416a015a323234394e5ede30ceeb664d979d795b403" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "5+\\pi "] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "596a51becfd398064bc85416a015a323234394e5ede30ceeb664d979d795b403" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "5-\\pi "] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "596a51becfd398064bc85416a015a323234394e5ede30ceeb664d979d795b403" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "9+\\pi "] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "596a51becfd398064bc85416a015a323234394e5ede30ceeb664d979d795b403" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "9-\\pi "] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/state.json" "file_sha256" "596a51becfd398064bc85416a015a323234394e5ede30ceeb664d979d795b403" "basis" 1227 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032967/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-117666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}7-3x, & x<-1 \\\\ 10, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}(7-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(7x-3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{-5}^{-1}+10x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[7(-1)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-7(-5)+3\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{2}]+10[(1)-(-1)] \\\\  & =[28+36]+10[2] \\\\  & =64+20 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-117666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "21"] ["math" "42"] ["math" "62"] ["math" "64"] ["math" "84"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "84"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-342011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1, \\\\ -2x+3, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-342011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{3}(-2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-1}^{1}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+3x){|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}-(\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3})]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3(3)-(-{1}^{2}+3(1))] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})]\\,+\\,[0-2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-342011" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-342011" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-342011" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{4}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+1, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[(0)-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}-(-2)+\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}]+[(2)^{2}+2-(0)^{2}-(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117669" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-117669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{14}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{16}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{20}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117669" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{16}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x+5, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+13, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}(\\frac{1}{3}x+13)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+5x){|}_{-1}^{3}+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+13x){|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =[3\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+5(3)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-5(-1)]+[\\frac{{9}^{2}}{6}+13(9)-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{6}-13(3)] \\\\  & =[12+20]+[12+78] \\\\  & =32+90 \\\\  & =122.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "101"] ["math" "1115"] ["math" "122"] ["math" "127"] ["math" "90"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117662" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "122"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-342008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+2, & x<0, \\\\ -2x+1, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-342008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}(\\frac{1}{2}x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+2x){|}_{-4}^{0}\\,+\\,(-{x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{4}{0}^{2}+2(0)-(\\frac{1}{4}(-4)^{2}+2(-4))]\\,+\\,[-{3}^{2}+3-(-{0}^{2}+0)] \\\\  & =[\\,0-(4-8)]\\,+\\,[\\,-9+3-0] \\\\  & =4-6 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-342008" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-342008" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-342008" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/f725b571954587b7b88426a254d7a6fc05f09b63ab2cc94b81303a510e8c1798.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,2),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [2,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,3].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-2+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-117702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-117702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-49168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/96622ea1476cf79ec3ff6d6486d77eda465de80d005304f542b4aadfb6be2b7a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-49168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =ab \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4.5-8 \\\\  & =-3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-49168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-49168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-49168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12.5"] ["math" "-3.5"] ["math" "12.5"] ["math" "3.5"] ["math" "4.5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-49168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3.5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/bac953cd2389ef36b415dcdce87730f244958ef0fe2f67911878c5cb3bfcafc6.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in (0,4],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [4,6].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [4,6].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =8+4-1 \\\\  & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "11"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86167" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/e9e1ba80436256409381b9d1c1762696156abe1cbfb516f03cfb4837ae2330b2.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-86168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86168" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/d43fa0efcfb8788316c86958b1175741c136588e81a8119131d1ca0f8d8ee229.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of four parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-2),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,-1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-1,1),$ and the line segments for $x\\in [1,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,-1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-18004" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/aa21323548eb2147949bac5bb379ed8fa6672d1b9fcb73137fad484c47e3cac9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,2)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [2,6].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,2).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,6].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-353385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4+2\\pi "] ["math" "4+\\pi "] ["math" "8+2\\pi "] ["math" "8+4\\pi "] ["math" "8+\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8+2\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-339328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/dcc3b282b99d1b74669ea47455f424361258e33497527bd69e29d50c5083aec4.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-339328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3),$ the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (-3,-1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [-1,4].$ Using the additive property of integrals, we write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-4.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-3,-1).$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=\\pi -4.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,4].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+\\pi -4+10 \\\\  & =2+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-339328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-339328" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-339328" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2+\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/3e0eca1a04396c0c86bbf5c3e5a9b3ff7071eacfe3d8c4281549e70cda809e8c.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a right triange, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a combination of an inverted quarter-circle of radius $2$ and a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =4-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =5+(4-\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4-\\pi "] ["math" "5+\\pi "] ["math" "5-\\pi "] ["math" "9+\\pi "] ["math" "9-\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-86174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9-\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/877771be920838a330edabe84b07eb817f1f3d7f1343b6866b9c2f078e079a67.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}b\\cdot h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-353387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi +16"] ["math" "2\\pi +4"] ["math" "2\\pi +8"] ["math" "4\\pi +8"] ["math" "\\pi +4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-353387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi +8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-339573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/be0329f2bc3722a6b3381dcfdbed212dc1e647f7f165cefb4c00f939b74b6d74.png)\n\nIf the graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above, then\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-339573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-3,1),$ the line segment for $x\\in [1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in [3,6].$ Therefore, we can use the additive property of integrals to write\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$   The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (2)^{2}+4(1) \\\\  & =2\\pi +4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,3].$   The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,6].$   The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi +4+2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi +3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-339573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "44eaaf3800c1b6c94900ec18255a896d49f8972354fb4ac184887250d3b64985"} {"question" "q-339573" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "q-339573" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{2}+2\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/state.json" "file_sha256" "890110ddef84d8e81c91a218dbcaff89a7ffdf5bcac976c00d3febd43b599054"} {"question" "e-432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^5 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+3, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-432.json" "file_sha256" "56e94a6e43f7791b16ce88d775bd5d24166385feb3298658bf6fba5ab6cec906"} {"question" "e-432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}3\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x{|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+3x){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =[3(0)-3(-2)]+[({5}^{2}+3(5))-({0}^{2}+3(0))] \\\\  & =6+25+15 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-432.json" "file_sha256" "56e94a6e43f7791b16ce88d775bd5d24166385feb3298658bf6fba5ab6cec906"} {"question" "e-433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/ad45cbdcd126df931f1a35c60ca12b45d520a2a0a5cddf4fa012c0515d3024c7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-433.json" "file_sha256" "c83c7637a9688f7b373c3effa8693da82a332617a4df56e089bd7963d894bb44"} {"question" "e-433" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [0,2)$ and the line segment for $x \\in [2,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{-A_2}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [2,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\int_{2}^{4} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x =-A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-433.json" "file_sha256" "c83c7637a9688f7b373c3effa8693da82a332617a4df56e089bd7963d894bb44"} {"question" "e-434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/36c6a1c84e0b0c32a5fd35fd422645f60340da6d205dd8cc31054cd558c2f5f9.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-4}^4 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-434.json" "file_sha256" "9265f0acf258503c6032c37b255ea2b1425b9d43803fc4bd6e02a8f526100f50"} {"question" "e-434" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The graph consists of four parts: the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-4,0],$ a line segment for $x\\in (0,2),$ a line segment for $x\\in (2,4],$ and the point at $x=2.$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+0+{{{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle with a radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = -A_2 = -2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in (2,4].$  The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi -2+2 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/example-434.json" "file_sha256" "9265f0acf2